export const MAIN_HTML = "<article class=\"hub\">\n\n<header class=\"hub-head\">\n<h1>Ceiling, Visibility, and Flight Categories: VFR, MVFR, IFR, and LIFR</h1>\n<p class=\"byline\">PlaneWX \u00b7 Last reviewed October 1, 2026</p>\n\n<p class=\"answer\">A ceiling is the lowest cloud layer reported as broken or overcast, or the vertical visibility into fog or another obscuration, always measured above ground level. Visibility is how far you can see and identify objects. Together they set the flight category: VFR, MVFR, IFR, or LIFR. Those categories are for situational awareness; your legal VFR minimums are in 14&nbsp;CFR&nbsp;91.155&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.4.2.14]</a><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2, para. 7&#8209;1&#8209;7]</a></span>.</p>\n\n<section class=\"takeaways\" aria-label=\"Key takeaways\">\n<h2>Key takeaways</h2>\n<ul>\n  <li>Read both numbers. The worse of ceiling and visibility sets the category&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2, para. 7&#8209;1&#8209;7]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li>A green dot means \u201cabove MVFR at that station.\u201d It doesn\u2019t mean the route meets your minimums or the legal ones&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, paras. 3.4.2.14 and 18.2.2]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li>Write your own ceiling and visibility numbers down, above the legal floor, and don\u2019t adjust them to make a trip work&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-6\">[6, ch. 2]</a></span>.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n</header>\n\n<aside class=\"keyfacts\" aria-label=\"Key facts about ceiling, visibility, and flight categories\">\n<details class=\"kf-box\" id=\"key-facts\" open>\n<summary><h2>Key facts</h2></summary>\n<ol>\n  <li>A <a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-ceiling\" data-gl=\"ceiling\">ceiling</a> is the lowest broken or overcast layer, or the vertical visibility into an obscuration&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-4\">[4]</a><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 24.4.3.9]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li>Ceilings are always above ground level&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2, para. 7&#8209;1&#8209;14]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li>Prevailing visibility covers at least half the horizon circle, not all of it&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2, para. 7&#8209;1&#8209;15]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li>LIFR: ceiling below 500&nbsp;ft and/or visibility below 1&nbsp;mile&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2, para. 7&#8209;1&#8209;7]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li>IFR: 500 to below 1,000&nbsp;ft and/or 1 to below 3&nbsp;miles&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2, para. 7&#8209;1&#8209;7]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li>MVFR: 1,000 to 3,000&nbsp;ft and/or 3 to 5&nbsp;miles inclusive&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2, para. 7&#8209;1&#8209;7]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li>VFR: above 3,000&nbsp;ft and above 5&nbsp;miles&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2, para. 7&#8209;1&#8209;7]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li>Flight categories are for situational awareness, not legal minimums&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.4.2.14]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li>About two-thirds of GA accidents in reduced visibility are fatal&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-5\">[5]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li>A valley station can report VFR while the ridges are in cloud&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 18.2.2]</a></span>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"kf-foot\">Hover, focus, or tap any dotted term for a definition and its official source.</p>\n<div class=\"kf-fade\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></div>\n</details>\n</aside>\n\n<div class=\"hub-body\">\n<p>Low ceilings and poor visibility are where VFR flights end badly. The NTSB: \u201cHistorically, about two-thirds of all general aviation (GA) accidents that occur in reduced-visibility weather conditions are fatal.\u201d Those accidents typically involve spatial disorientation or controlled flight into terrain&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-5\">[5]</a></span>. This page explains exactly what a ceiling is, how <a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-visibility\" data-gl=\"visibility\">Visibility</a> is measured and coded, what the four <a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-flightcat\" data-gl=\"flightcat\">flight categories</a> mean, and why none of them is a substitute for your own minimums.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"ceiling\">What is a ceiling?</h2>\n<p>The AIM: \u201cCeiling, by definition in the CFRs and as used in aviation weather reports and forecasts, is the height above ground (or water) level of the lowest layer of clouds or obscuring phenomenon that is reported as \u2018broken,\u2019 \u2018overcast,\u2019 or \u2018obscuration,\u2019\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2, para. 7&#8209;1&#8209;14]</a></span> Three consequences follow:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Few and scattered layers are not ceilings.</strong> \u201cThe ceiling is the lowest layer aloft reported as broken or overcast.\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 24.4.3.9]</a></span></li>\n  <li><strong>Fog can be the ceiling.</strong> \u201cIf the sky is totally obscured with ground-based clouds, the vertical visibility is the ceiling.\u201d That is the <a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-indefceil\" data-gl=\"indefceil\">indefinite ceiling</a>, coded <a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-vv\" data-gl=\"vv\">VV</a>&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 24.4.3.9]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Always AGL.</strong> \u201c(\u2018Ceiling\u2019 heights are always above ground level.)\u201d But a BKN030 in an area forecast is MSL, and PIREP heights are usually MSL&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2, para. 7&#8209;1&#8209;14]</a></span>. Check which reference a product uses.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>An indefinite ceiling is worse than a cloud deck at the same height. The Handbook explains that an obscuration limits slant range visibility, so on an approach \u201cThis pilot would be able to see the ground but not the runway.\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 24.4.3.9]</a></span></p>\n<p>One more code trap: \u201cCLR is used by automated stations to indicate no layers are detected at or below 12,000&nbsp;ft.\u201d SKC means a human observer saw no layers&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 24.4.3.9]</a></span>.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"visibility\">How is visibility measured and reported?</h2>\n<p>\u201cIt is defined as the greatest horizontal distance at which selected objects can be seen and identified, or its equivalent derived from instrumental measurements.\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 24.4.3.6]</a></span> A human observer reports <a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-pvis\" data-gl=\"pvis\">prevailing visibility</a>: \u201cPrevailing visibility is the greatest visibility equaled or exceeded throughout at least one half of the horizon circle, not necessarily contiguous.\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2, para. 7&#8209;1&#8209;15]</a></span> An automated station reports what its sensor measures&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 24.4.3.6]</a></span>.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Half the circle can be worse.</strong> A sector with significantly different visibility may only appear in the remarks&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2, para. 7&#8209;1&#8209;15]</a></span>. Read them.</li>\n  <li><strong>Tower and surface values.</strong> When either is below 4&nbsp;miles, the tower takes its own observation, and \u201cThe lower of these two values will be used as the prevailing visibility for aircraft operations.\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2, para. 7&#8209;1&#8209;15]</a></span></li>\n  <li><strong>\u201cLess than.\u201d</strong> \u201cU.S. automated stations use an M to indicate \u2018less than.\u2019\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 24.4.3.6]</a></span></li>\n  <li><strong>Units.</strong> U.S. reports use statute miles; \u201cMost other countries use meters.\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 24.4.3.6]</a></span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ground visibility is not flight visibility. Surface visibility is measured from the ground&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 24.4.3.6]</a></span>; what you see from the cockpit, through haze toward the sun or into rain, can be much less.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"categories\">What are the flight categories?</h2>\n<p>The AIM defines four categorical terms for reported or forecast ceiling and visibility, and the Handbook lists the color codes most weather websites use&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2, para. 7&#8209;1&#8209;7]</a><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.4.2.14]</a></span>:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"stack\" role=\"table\">\n<thead role=\"rowgroup\"><tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"col\" role=\"columnheader\">Category</th><th scope=\"col\" role=\"columnheader\">Ceiling</th><th scope=\"col\" role=\"columnheader\">Visibility</th><th scope=\"col\" role=\"columnheader\">Map color</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody role=\"rowgroup\">\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\"><a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-lifr\" data-gl=\"lifr\">LIFR</a></th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Ceiling\">Less than 500&nbsp;feet</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Visibility\">Less than 1&nbsp;mile</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Map color\">Purple</td></tr>\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\"><a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-ifr\" data-gl=\"ifr\">IFR</a></th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Ceiling\">500 to less than 1,000&nbsp;feet</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Visibility\">1 to less than 3&nbsp;miles</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Map color\">Red</td></tr>\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\"><a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-mvfr\" data-gl=\"mvfr\">MVFR</a></th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Ceiling\">1,000 to 3,000&nbsp;feet</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Visibility\">3 to 5&nbsp;miles inclusive</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Map color\">Blue</td></tr>\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\"><a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-vfr\" data-gl=\"vfr\">VFR</a></th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Ceiling\">Greater than 3,000&nbsp;feet</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Visibility\">Greater than 5&nbsp;miles</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Map color\">Green</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>When winds or gusts of 25&nbsp;knots or more are forecast for the outlook period, the word \u201cWIND\u201d is added to the category, even VFR&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2, para. 7&#8209;1&#8209;7]</a></span>. The FAA Risk Management Handbook uses the same four bands as the first step in setting personal minimums&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-6\">[6, ch. 2, Figure 2-1]</a></span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"worked\">Worked example: classify five reports</h3>\n<p>Apply the bands to the ceiling and visibility in each report extract. The worse of the two decides:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"stack\" role=\"table\">\n<thead role=\"rowgroup\"><tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"col\" role=\"columnheader\">Report extract</th><th scope=\"col\" role=\"columnheader\">Ceiling</th><th scope=\"col\" role=\"columnheader\">Visibility</th><th scope=\"col\" role=\"columnheader\">Category, and why</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody role=\"rowgroup\">\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\"><code>6SM SCT009 BKN040</code></th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Ceiling\">4,000&nbsp;ft. The scattered layer at 900&nbsp;ft is not a ceiling</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Visibility\">6&nbsp;SM</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Category, and why\">VFR. Ceiling over 3,000&nbsp;ft and visibility over 5&nbsp;miles&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2, paras. 7&#8209;1&#8209;7 and 7&#8209;1&#8209;14]</a></span></td></tr>\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\"><code>4SM BR BKN008</code></th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Ceiling\">800&nbsp;ft</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Visibility\">4&nbsp;SM</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Category, and why\">IFR, because of the ceiling. Use the worse of the two&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2, para. 7&#8209;1&#8209;7]</a></span></td></tr>\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\"><code>2SM BR OVC035</code></th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Ceiling\">3,500&nbsp;ft</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Visibility\">2&nbsp;SM</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Category, and why\">IFR, because of the visibility, even though the ceiling is VFR&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2, para. 7&#8209;1&#8209;7]</a></span></td></tr>\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\"><code>1/4SM FG VV003</code></th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Ceiling\">300&nbsp;ft, an <a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-indefceil\" data-gl=\"indefceil\">indefinite ceiling</a></td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Visibility\">1/4&nbsp;SM</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Category, and why\">LIFR. Vertical visibility into fog counts as the ceiling&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 24.4.3.9]</a></span></td></tr>\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\"><code>5SM HZ OVC030</code></th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Ceiling\">3,000&nbsp;ft</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Visibility\">5&nbsp;SM</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Category, and why\">MVFR. Both values sit at the top of the MVFR band, which is inclusive&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2, para. 7&#8209;1&#8209;7]</a><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.4.2.15]</a></span></td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n<h2 id=\"not-minimums\">Why isn\u2019t a VFR category the same as legal VFR?</h2>\n<p>The Handbook is explicit: \u201cThe VFR Weather Flight Category is not to be confused with the basic VFR weather minimums given in 14&nbsp;CFR&nbsp;\u00a7&nbsp;91.155. Weather Flight Categories are only intended for situational awareness.\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.4.2.14]</a></span> And for MVFR, \u201cWhile the \u2018R\u2019 in the acronym means \u2018rules,\u2019 there are no 14&nbsp;CFR&nbsp;part&nbsp;91 MVFR weather minimums.\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.4.2.15]</a></span></p>\n<p>The rule that governs you is 14&nbsp;CFR&nbsp;91.155. A few rows of its table&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-3\">[3, para. (a)]</a></span>:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"stack\" role=\"table\">\n<thead role=\"rowgroup\"><tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"col\" role=\"columnheader\">Where you are</th><th scope=\"col\" role=\"columnheader\">Flight visibility</th><th scope=\"col\" role=\"columnheader\">Distance from clouds</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody role=\"rowgroup\">\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\">Class E, below 10,000&nbsp;feet MSL</th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Flight visibility\">3&nbsp;statute miles</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Distance from clouds\">500&nbsp;feet below, 1,000&nbsp;feet above, 2,000&nbsp;feet horizontal</td></tr>\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\">Class G, 1,200&nbsp;feet or less above the surface, day (airplanes)</th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Flight visibility\">1&nbsp;statute mile</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Distance from clouds\">Clear of clouds</td></tr>\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\">Class G, 1,200&nbsp;feet or less above the surface, night (airplanes)</th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Flight visibility\">3&nbsp;statute miles</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Distance from clouds\">500&nbsp;feet below, 1,000&nbsp;feet above, 2,000&nbsp;feet horizontal</td></tr>\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\">Class B</th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Flight visibility\">3&nbsp;statute miles</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Distance from clouds\">Clear of clouds</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Two more parts matter on a low day. Under 91.155(c), \u201cno person may operate an aircraft beneath the ceiling under VFR within the lateral boundaries of controlled airspace designated to the surface for an airport when the ceiling is less than 1,000&nbsp;feet.\u201d Under 91.155(d), taking off, landing, or entering the pattern under VFR in a surface area needs ground visibility of at least 3&nbsp;statute miles, or flight visibility of 3&nbsp;miles if ground visibility isn\u2019t reported&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-3\">[3, paras. (c) and (d)]</a></span>. Read the full table in the regulation; it has night, altitude, and Class G exceptions.</p>\n<p>The reverse trap is just as real. A field can be legal VFR and still be a bad place to fly: a 1,500 foot ceiling over hills, or 3&nbsp;miles in haze at night.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"mountains\">Why can a station report VFR while the route is in cloud?</h2>\n<p>Reports describe a point. \u201cThe large elevation variations around mountains can cause surface weather observations to mislead. For example, a weather station located in a valley could report a VFR cloud ceiling, while a hiker in the mountains sees fog.\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 18.2.2]</a></span> <a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-sierra\" data-gl=\"sierra\">AIRMET Sierra</a> covers widespread <a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-mtnobsc\" data-gl=\"mtnobsc\">mountain obscuration</a>&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, Table 3-16]</a></span>. AC&nbsp;91&#8209;92 warns pilots not to \u201cForget that areas en route, or even near airports, may be below VFR minimums, even though reporting stations are at or near VFR minimums.\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-7\">[7, para. 8.2]</a></span> The <a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/mountain-wave\">mountain weather guide</a> covers terrain effects, and <a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/fog\">the fog guide</a> covers why visibility can collapse at dawn.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"field-reference\">Where are the official ceiling and visibility definitions?</h2>\n<section class=\"fieldref\" aria-labelledby=\"fieldref-h\">\n<h3 id=\"fieldref-h\">Official ceiling and visibility definitions</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Start here:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/aim_html/chap7_section_1.html#7-1-7\">AIM&nbsp;7&#8209;1&#8209;7, Categorical Ceiling and Visibility Conditions</a>, <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/aim_html/chap7_section_1.html#7-1-14\">7&#8209;1&#8209;14, Reporting of Cloud Heights</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/aim_html/chap7_section_1.html#7-1-15\">7&#8209;1&#8209;15, Reporting Prevailing Visibility</a>&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/FAA-H-8083-28B.pdf#page=295\">FAA Aviation Weather Handbook, Sections 24.4.3.6 and 24.4.3.9, visibility and sky condition</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/FAA-H-8083-28B.pdf#page=51\">Section 3.4.2.14, flight categories</a>&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-14/chapter-I/subchapter-F/part-91/subpart-B/section-91.155\">14&nbsp;CFR&nbsp;91.155, Basic VFR weather minimums</a>&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-3\">[3]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"https://www.ntsb.gov/Advocacy/safety-alerts/Documents/SA-020.pdf\">NTSB Safety Alert SA&#8209;020, Reduced Visual References Require Vigilance</a>&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-5\">[5]</a></span>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"status\">Links checked October 1, 2026: all returned HTTP 200.</p>\n</section>\n\n<h2>Where do ceiling and visibility reports mislead pilots?</h2>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Scattered isn\u2019t harmless.</strong> It isn\u2019t a ceiling, but it can be a wall at your altitude&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 24.4.3.9]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li><strong>CLR at an automated station.</strong> Nothing detected at or below 12,000&nbsp;ft is not the same as clear&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 24.4.3.9]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Half the horizon.</strong> Prevailing visibility can hide a bad sector&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2, para. 7&#8209;1&#8209;15]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Point reports in terrain.</strong> Valley stations can\u2019t see the ridges&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 18.2.2]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Color dots as permission.</strong> Categories are for awareness only&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.4.2.14]</a></span>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>How do you use ceiling and visibility in a go/no-go decision?</h2>\n<p>The decision is yours as PIC. The FAA\u2019s personal minimums process gives you numbers to hold yourself to:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Set a baseline above the legal floor.</strong> The Risk Management Handbook\u2019s sample baseline uses a 5,000 foot ceiling and 8&nbsp;miles visibility for night VFR. Build yours from your own experience&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-6\">[6, ch. 2, Figure 2-8]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Add margin when conditions stack up.</strong> Its adjustment table suggests adding at least 500&nbsp;feet to the ceiling and at least 1/2&nbsp;mile to visibility for factors like fatigue, an unfamiliar airplane, or an unfamiliar airport&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-6\">[6, ch. 2, Figure 2-9]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Don\u2019t move the numbers under pressure.</strong> The pilot should \u201cNot adjust personal minimums to complete a specific flight.\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-6\">[6, ch. 2]</a></span></li>\n  <li><strong>More margin at night.</strong> AC&nbsp;91&#8209;92: \u201cAllow more margin for weather at night.\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-7\">[7, para. 8.1]</a></span></li>\n  <li><strong>Have the out before you need it.</strong> \u201cPlan ahead with cancellation or diversion alternatives.\u201d And \u201cDon\u2019t allow a situation to become dangerous before deciding to act.\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-5\">[5]</a></span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>More on setting numbers: the PlaneWX guide to <a href=\"https://www.planewx.ai/blog/personal-minimums-weather-planning\">personal minimums for weather planning</a> and the <a href=\"https://www.planewx.ai/blog/vfr-weather-go-no-go\">VFR weather go/no-go guide</a>.</p>\n\n<h2>Where do ceiling and visibility fit in a disciplined decision?</h2>\n<p>PlaneWX is decision support for general aviation pilots, built around one idea: \u201cFly like it\u2019s your job.\u201d Treat the weather decision the way a professional crew would, with the same process every time. That process is a loop: <span class=\"loop\">Brief, then FRAT, then Fly or Stay, then Debrief</span>, with a Mentor if you want one. Ceiling and visibility feed the Brief. Here\u2019s how PlaneWX handles them, as its help center describes&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-8\">[8]</a></span>:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Brief: against your numbers.</strong> You set a comfort limit and a hard limit for ceiling and visibility, separately for VFR and IFR. Below the hard limit at departure or arrival is a deduction of up to 40 points each; within 500&nbsp;ft or 1&nbsp;SM of the limit is a smaller caution (<a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/scoring\">Scoring</a>, <a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/personal-minimums\">Personal Minimums</a>).</li>\n  <li><strong>Brief: along the route.</strong> On a VFR trip, an IFR G-AIRMET within 25&nbsp;NM of the route is a hard stop, and G-AIRMET Sierra mountain obscuration on the route is a large deduction (<a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/scoring\">Scoring</a>).</li>\n  <li><strong>Brief: categories and units.</strong> Enhanced view shows VFR, MVFR, IFR, and LIFR indicators. Showing visibility in meters is a display preference only; scoring and stored minimums stay in statute miles (<a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/understanding-briefings\">Understanding Your Briefing</a>, <a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/visibility-meters\">Show Visibility in Meters</a>).</li>\n  <li><strong>FRAT.</strong> The flight risk assessment opens in the last 4&nbsp;hours before departure (<a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/frat\">FRAT</a>).</li>\n  <li><strong>Fly or Stay.</strong> \u201cPlaneWX never recommends GO or NO-GO. You make the call as PIC.\u201d (<a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/risk-loop\">The Risk Loop</a>)</li>\n  <li><strong>Debrief.</strong> Compare the visibility you saw with what was reported. Self Debrief is a PlaneWX Labs feature for Pro Plus (<a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/risk-loop\">The Risk Loop</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The help center is plain about the limits: \u201cPlaneWX is not a substitute for a complete, independent pre-flight weather briefing. Pull one from a source you already trust before you fly.\u201d (<a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/risk-loop\">The Risk Loop</a>)</p>\n\n<!-- Screenshot slots (dark mode; Chief of Staff review required before adding; real trips only, blur tail numbers and names):\n     S1: Ceilings & Visibility grid with flight category indicators.\n     S2: Personal Minimums bar showing comfort and hard ceiling limits. -->\n\n<p><a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/\">Check ceilings and visibility against your own minimums in a PlaneWX briefing</a>, or read <a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/personal-minimums\">how PlaneWX uses personal minimums</a>. Related: <a href=\"https://www.planewx.ai/news/visibility-in-meters\">visibility in meters</a>.</p>\n\n<nav class=\"hub-related\" aria-labelledby=\"related-h\">\n<h2 id=\"related-h\">More from the PlaneWX Learning Center</h2>\n<ul>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/taf\">What Is a TAF? How to Read a Terminal Aerodrome Forecast</a>. The airport forecast, with FM, TEMPO, PROB30, and a full worked decode.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/metar\">What Is a METAR? How to Read an Airport Weather Report</a>. The airport observation, every group decoded, plus when a SPECI is issued.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/airmet-sigmet\">AIRMET, SIGMET, and CWA: In-Flight Weather Advisories Explained</a>. G-AIRMETs, SIGMETs, Convective SIGMETs, and Center Weather Advisories, with decoded samples.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/pirep\">What Is a PIREP? How to Read and Give a Pilot Weather Report</a>. The UA and UUA format, the icing and turbulence scales, and how to give one.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/winds-aloft\">Winds and Temperatures Aloft: How to Read an FB Forecast</a>. The FB code decoded, including winds over 100&nbsp;knots, and how model winds differ.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/icing\">Icing Forecasts: CIP, FIP, Freezing Level, and Icing Intensity</a>. What the icing products show, what their intensity words mean, and where they fall short.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/turbulence\">Turbulence Forecasts: GTG, GTG-N, and the Intensity Scale</a>. The automated turbulence forecast and nowcast, EDR, and the advisories that cover what GTG does not.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/weather-radar\">Weather Radar for Pilots: NEXRAD, TDWR, Reflectivity, and Datalink Delay</a>. How to read radar images, their blind spots, and why cockpit mosaics are older than they look.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/density-altitude\">Density Altitude Calculator and Guide: What It Is and How to Calculate It</a>. The FAA definition, the formulas, a worked example, a calculator, and the Koch chart.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/flight-risk-assessment-tool\">Flight Risk Assessment Tool (FRAT): What It Is and How to Use One Honestly</a>. Where the FAA FRAT comes from, how the scores work, and how to fill one out without fooling yourself.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/thunderstorms\">Thunderstorms for Pilots: Hazards, Avoidance, Outlooks, and Watches</a>. Why every thunderstorm is a hazard, the 20-mile rule, and the convective outlook, TCF, ECFP, and watch products.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/fog\">Fog for Pilots: Radiation, Advection, Upslope, and Freezing Fog</a>. How each type of fog forms and clears, how it is reported and forecast, and what to do when the spread is small.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/wind-shear-microburst\">Low-Level Wind Shear and Microbursts: Recognition, Reports, and Escape</a>. The LLWS definition, microburst structure, the alerts you hear on ATIS, and the FAA escape guidance.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/mountain-wave\">Mountain Wave and Mountain Weather: Lee Waves, Rotors, and Downslope Winds</a>. When mountain waves form, the clouds that mark them, rotor and downslope hazards, and how to plan a crossing.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/weather-briefings\">Weather Briefing Types: Standard, Abbreviated, and Outlook Briefings</a>. What each FAA briefing type contains, when to get each one, and how a self-briefing follows the same order.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/weather-risk\">Weather Risk and Personal Minimums: FAA Definitions and Where to Go Next</a>. Hazard, risk, and personal minimums as the FAA defines them, with links to the PlaneWX guides that go deeper.</li>\n</ul>\n</nav>\n\n<section class=\"faq\">\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n\n<h3>What counts as a ceiling?</h3>\n<p>The lowest layer reported as broken or overcast, or the vertical visibility into a surface-based obscuration. The Pilot/Controller Glossary defines it as \u201cThe heights above the earth\u2019s surface of the lowest layer of clouds or obscuring phenomena that is reported as \u2018broken,\u2019 \u2018overcast,\u2019 or \u2018obscuration,\u2019 and not classified as \u2018thin\u2019 or \u2018partial.\u2019\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-4\">[4]</a></span></p>\n\n<h3>Is a scattered layer a ceiling?</h3>\n<p>No. Only broken, overcast, or an obscuration counts&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-4\">[4]</a><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 24.4.3.9]</a></span>. A scattered layer can still block your path, so read every layer.</p>\n\n<h3>Are ceilings above ground level or sea level?</h3>\n<p>Above ground. The AIM: \u201c(\u2018Ceiling\u2019 heights are always above ground level.)\u201d Cloud heights in an area forecast can be MSL, and pilot reports are usually MSL&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2, para. 7&#8209;1&#8209;14]</a></span>.</p>\n\n<h3>What is prevailing visibility?</h3>\n<p>\u201cPrevailing visibility is the greatest visibility equaled or exceeded throughout at least one half of the horizon circle, not necessarily contiguous.\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2, para. 7&#8209;1&#8209;15]</a></span> The rest of the circle can be worse.</p>\n\n<h3>What are the ceiling and visibility limits for MVFR?</h3>\n<p>Ceiling 1,000 to 3,000&nbsp;feet and/or visibility 3 to 5&nbsp;miles, inclusive&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2, para. 7&#8209;1&#8209;7]</a></span>. The Handbook notes \u201cthere are no 14&nbsp;CFR&nbsp;part&nbsp;91 MVFR weather minimums\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.4.2.15]</a></span>.</p>\n\n<h3>Is a VFR flight category the same as VFR weather minimums?</h3>\n<p>No. The Handbook says the VFR category \u201cis not to be confused with the basic VFR weather minimums given in 14&nbsp;CFR&nbsp;\u00a7&nbsp;91.155. Weather Flight Categories are only intended for situational awareness.\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.4.2.14]</a></span></p>\n\n<h3>What does M1/4SM mean?</h3>\n<p>\u201cU.S. automated stations use an M to indicate \u2018less than.\u2019\u201d M1/4SM is less than a quarter mile&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 24.4.3.6]</a></span>.</p>\n\n<h3>Why does CLR not mean the sky is clear?</h3>\n<p>\u201cCLR is used by automated stations to indicate no layers are detected at or below 12,000&nbsp;ft.\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 24.4.3.9]</a></span> There can be clouds above that.</p>\n\n<h3>How does PlaneWX use my ceiling and visibility minimums?</h3>\n<p>Its help center says a forecast ceiling or visibility below your hard limit at departure or arrival is a deduction of up to 40 points each, and within 500&nbsp;ft or 1&nbsp;SM of a limit is a smaller caution. PlaneWX never recommends GO or NO-GO; you make the call as PIC&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-8\">[8]</a></span>.</p>\n</section>\n\n<section class=\"glossary\" id=\"glossary\" aria-labelledby=\"glossary-h\">\n<h2 id=\"glossary-h\">Glossary</h2>\n<p>Every term below links to the FAA, NWS, or NOAA document that defines it. The same definitions appear when you hover, focus, or tap a dotted term on this page.</p>\n<dl>\n  <dt id=\"gl-sierra\"><dfn>AIRMET Sierra</dfn></dt>\n  <dd>The AIRMET series for IFR conditions and extensive mountain obscuration. <span class=\"src\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/FAA-H-8083-28B.pdf#page=383\">FAA Aviation Weather Handbook, 26.3.1.2</a></span></dd>\n  <dt id=\"gl-ceiling\"><dfn>Ceiling</dfn></dt>\n  <dd>The lowest broken or overcast layer, or the vertical visibility into an obscuration. VV008, BKN008, and OVC008 all mean an 800&nbsp;ft ceiling. <span class=\"src\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.weather.gov/media/directives/010_pdfs/pd01008013curr.pdf#page=21\">NWSI&nbsp;10&#8209;813, Appendix B2.7.1</a></span></dd>\n  <dt id=\"gl-flightcat\"><dfn>Flight category</dfn></dt>\n  <dd>A color-coded label of VFR, MVFR, IFR or LIFR that summarizes ceiling and visibility for situational awareness only. <span class=\"src\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/FAA-H-8083-28B.pdf#page=51\">FAA Aviation Weather Handbook, 3.4.2.14</a></span></dd>\n  <dt id=\"gl-ifr\"><dfn>IFR</dfn> (Instrument flight rules): flight category</dt>\n  <dd>Ceiling 500 to less than 1,000&nbsp;feet and/or visibility 1 to less than 3&nbsp;miles. <span class=\"src\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/aim_html/chap7_section_1.html#7-1-7\">AIM, 7&#8209;1&#8209;7</a></span></dd>\n  <dt id=\"gl-indefceil\"><dfn>Indefinite ceiling</dfn></dt>\n  <dd>A ceiling reported as vertical visibility into a surface-based obscuration, such as fog, coded VV in a METAR. <span class=\"src\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/FAA-H-8083-28B.pdf#page=299\">FAA Aviation Weather Handbook, 24.4.3.9</a></span></dd>\n  <dt id=\"gl-lifr\"><dfn>LIFR</dfn> (Low IFR): flight category</dt>\n  <dd>Ceiling below 500&nbsp;feet and/or visibility below 1&nbsp;mile. <span class=\"src\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/aim_html/chap7_section_1.html#7-1-7\">AIM, 7&#8209;1&#8209;7</a></span></dd>\n  <dt id=\"gl-mtnobsc\"><dfn>Mountain obscuration</dfn></dt>\n  <dd>A condition in which mountains or ridges are hidden by clouds, precipitation, smoke, or other obscurations. <span class=\"src\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/FAA-H-8083-28B.pdf#page=232\">FAA Aviation Weather Handbook, 18.2.2</a></span></dd>\n  <dt id=\"gl-mvfr\"><dfn>MVFR</dfn> (Marginal VFR): flight category</dt>\n  <dd>Ceiling 1,000 to 3,000&nbsp;feet and/or visibility 3 to 5&nbsp;miles. <span class=\"src\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/aim_html/chap7_section_1.html#7-1-7\">AIM, 7&#8209;1&#8209;7</a></span></dd>\n  <dt id=\"gl-pvis\"><dfn>Prevailing visibility</dfn></dt>\n  <dd>The greatest distance that can be seen throughout at least half of the horizon circle, not necessarily in one continuous sector. <span class=\"src\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/FAA-H-8083-28B.pdf#page=295\">FAA Aviation Weather Handbook, 24.4.3.6</a></span></dd>\n  <dt id=\"gl-vfr\"><dfn>VFR</dfn> (Visual flight rules): flight category</dt>\n  <dd>Ceiling greater than 3,000&nbsp;feet and visibility greater than 5&nbsp;miles. <span class=\"src\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/aim_html/chap7_section_1.html#7-1-7\">AIM, 7&#8209;1&#8209;7</a></span></dd>\n  <dt id=\"gl-visibility\"><dfn>Visibility</dfn></dt>\n  <dd>The greatest horizontal distance at which selected objects can be seen and identified, or its instrument equivalent. <span class=\"src\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/FAA-H-8083-28B.pdf#page=295\">FAA Aviation Weather Handbook, 24.4.3.6</a></span></dd>\n  <dt id=\"gl-vv\"><dfn>VV</dfn> (Vertical visibility)</dt>\n  <dd>How far up you can see into a surface-based obscuration such as fog, in hundreds of feet; it counts as the ceiling. <span class=\"src\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.weather.gov/media/directives/010_pdfs/pd01008013curr.pdf#page=21\">NWSI&nbsp;10&#8209;813, Appendix B2.7.2</a></span></dd>\n</dl>\n</section>\n\n<p class=\"note\">This page explains published FAA and NTSB guidance. It is not a substitute for the full text of 14&nbsp;CFR&nbsp;91.155 or for a current weather briefing. Always get a current briefing before you fly.</p>\n\n<section class=\"refs\" aria-labelledby=\"references\">\n<h2 id=\"references\">References</h2>\n<ol>\n  <li id=\"ref-1\">FAA&#8209;H&#8209;8083&#8209;28B, \u201cAviation Weather Handbook.\u201d Federal Aviation Administration, Flight Standards Service. April 2, 2026. Paragraphs cited: 3.4.2.13 (Table 3-16), 3.4.2.14, 3.4.2.15, 18.2.2, 24.4.3.6, 24.4.3.9. <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/regulationspolicies/handbooksmanuals/aviation/faa-h-8083-28b-aviation-weather-handbook\">https://www.faa.gov/regulationspolicies/handbooksmanuals/aviation/faa-h-8083-28b-aviation-weather-handbook</a>. PDF: <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/FAA-H-8083-28B.pdf\">https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/FAA-H-8083-28B.pdf</a></li>\n  <li id=\"ref-2\">\u201c7&#8209;1&#8209;7. Categorical Ceiling and Visibility Conditions,\u201d \u201c7&#8209;1&#8209;14. Reporting of Cloud Heights,\u201d and \u201c7&#8209;1&#8209;15. Reporting Prevailing Visibility.\u201d Aeronautical Information Manual (AIM), Chapter 7, Section 1. Federal Aviation Administration. Effective July 9, 2026 (Change 3). <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/aim_html/chap7_section_1.html\">https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/aim_html/chap7_section_1.html</a></li>\n  <li id=\"ref-3\">14&nbsp;CFR&nbsp;91.155, \u201cBasic VFR weather minimums.\u201d Electronic Code of Federal Regulations, current as of September 15, 2026. <a href=\"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-14/chapter-I/subchapter-F/part-91/subpart-B/section-91.155\">https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-14/chapter-I/subchapter-F/part-91/subpart-B/section-91.155</a></li>\n  <li id=\"ref-4\">\u201cCEILING.\u201d Pilot/Controller Glossary. Federal Aviation Administration. Basic with Changes 1, 2, and 3, July 9, 2026. <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/media/PCG_Bsc_w_Chg_1_2_and_3_dtd_7-9-26.pdf\">https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/media/PCG_Bsc_w_Chg_1_2_and_3_dtd_7-9-26.pdf</a></li>\n  <li id=\"ref-5\">NTSB Safety Alert SA&#8209;020, \u201cReduced Visual References Require Vigilance.\u201d National Transportation Safety Board. March 2013, revised December 2015. <a href=\"https://www.ntsb.gov/Advocacy/safety-alerts/Documents/SA-020.pdf\">https://www.ntsb.gov/Advocacy/safety-alerts/Documents/SA-020.pdf</a></li>\n  <li id=\"ref-6\">FAA&#8209;H&#8209;8083&#8209;2A, \u201cRisk Management Handbook.\u201d Federal Aviation Administration. 2022. Chapter 2, personal minimums (Figures 2-1, 2-8, and 2-9). <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/regulationspolicies/handbooksmanuals/risk-management-handbook-faa-h-8083-2a\">https://www.faa.gov/regulationspolicies/handbooksmanuals/risk-management-handbook-faa-h-8083-2a</a></li>\n  <li id=\"ref-7\">AC&nbsp;91&#8209;92, \u201cPilot\u2019s Guide to a Preflight Briefing.\u201d Federal Aviation Administration. March 15, 2021 (editorial update March 19, 2025). Paragraphs cited: 8.1, 8.2. <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/documentLibrary/media/Advisory_Circular/AC_91-92_Ed_Upd_(3-19-25).pdf\">https://www.faa.gov/documentLibrary/media/Advisory_Circular/AC_91-92_Ed_Upd_(3-19-25).pdf</a></li>\n  <li id=\"ref-8\">PlaneWX Help Center: \u201cScoring,\u201d \u201cPersonal Minimums,\u201d \u201cUnderstanding Your Briefing,\u201d \u201cShow Visibility in Meters,\u201d \u201cFRAT,\u201d \u201cThe Risk Loop.\u201d PlaneWX. Accessed October 1, 2026. <a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/scoring\">Scoring</a> \u00b7 <a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/personal-minimums\">Personal Minimums</a> \u00b7 <a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/understanding-briefings\">Understanding Your Briefing</a> \u00b7 <a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/visibility-meters\">Show Visibility in Meters</a> \u00b7 <a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/frat\">FRAT</a> \u00b7 <a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/risk-loop\">The Risk Loop</a></li>\n</ol>\n</section>\n</div>\n\n</article>"
export const INLINE_STYLE = "\n  :root {\n    --bg: #ffffff; --fg: #1b1f24; --muted: #57606a; --line: #d0d7de;\n    --card: #f6f8fa; --accent: #0b5cad; --quote: #eef4fb;\n  }\n  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {\n    :root {\n      --bg: #0f1419; --fg: #e6e8eb; --muted: #9aa4af; --line: #2d3640;\n      --card: #161d24; --accent: #6cb4ff; --quote: #142231;\n    }\n  }\n  * { box-sizing: border-box; }\n  html { -webkit-text-size-adjust: 100%; }\n  body { margin: 0; background: var(--bg); color: var(--fg);\n    font: 18px/1.65 -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, \"Segoe UI\", Roboto, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif; }\n  main { max-width: 760px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 32px 20px 64px; }\n  h1 { font-size: 2.05rem; line-height: 1.2; margin: 0 0 12px; }\n  h2 { font-size: 1.45rem; line-height: 1.3; margin: 44px 0 12px; padding-top: 8px; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }\n  h3 { font-size: 1.12rem; margin: 26px 0 8px; }\n  p, ul, ol { margin: 0 0 16px; }\n  li { margin: 0 0 6px; }\n  a { color: var(--accent); }\n  .byline { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.92rem; margin-bottom: 24px; }\n  .answer { background: var(--card); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-left: 4px solid var(--accent);\n    border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px 18px; font-size: 1.06rem; margin: 0 0 20px; }\n  .takeaways { background: var(--card); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px 4px; margin: 0 0 24px; }\n  .takeaways h2 { border: 0; margin: 0 0 8px; padding: 0; font-size: 1.05rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .04em; color: var(--muted); }\n  blockquote { margin: 0 0 18px; padding: 12px 16px; background: var(--quote); border-left: 4px solid var(--accent); border-radius: 6px; }\n  blockquote p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }\n  blockquote footer { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: 6px; }\n  .cite { font-size: 0.78em; vertical-align: super; line-height: 0; text-decoration: none; white-space: nowrap; }\n  .cite:hover { text-decoration: underline; }\n  .cite + .cite { margin-left: 0.25em; }\n  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 0 0 20px; font-size: 0.95rem; }\n  th, td { border: 1px solid var(--line); padding: 10px 12px; vertical-align: top; text-align: left; }\n  th { background: var(--card); }\n  .checklist { background: var(--card); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px 6px; margin: 0 0 20px; }\n  .checklist h3 { margin-top: 10px; }\n  .checklist ul { list-style: none; padding-left: 0; }\n  .checklist li::before { content: \"\\2610\"; margin-right: 8px; color: var(--muted); }\n  .faq h3 { margin-top: 22px; }\n  .note { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.95rem; font-style: italic; }\n  .refs ol { padding-left: 1.4em; font-size: 0.93rem; }\n  .refs li { margin-bottom: 10px; word-break: break-word; }\n  .refs li:target { background: var(--quote); outline: 2px solid var(--accent); border-radius: 4px; }\n  .loop { font-weight: 600; }\n  /* ---- Hub layout: header, Key Facts sidebar, body (PlaneWX /learn/aviation-weather template) ---- */\n  main.hub-main { max-width: 1120px; }\n  .hub { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 0 40px; }\n  .hub-head, .keyfacts, .hub-body { min-width: 0; }\n  /* ---- Sub-headings: real h3, semibold, about 1.13x body, clear space above ---- */\n  .hub h3 { font-size: 1.13em; font-weight: 600; line-height: 1.35; margin: 36px 0 10px; }\n  .hub .faq h3 { margin-top: 32px; }\n  .hub .fieldref h3 { margin-top: 4px; }\n  /* ---- Citations stay on the line of the word before them ---- */\n  /* A cite group never breaks inside, at any width (including Key Facts). Groups too long for the narrowest\n     container are split in the source so each cite sits right after its claim. */\n  .cites { white-space: nowrap; }\n  .cite + .cite { margin-left: 0; }  /* no gap before a cite that follows a cite */\n  /* ---- Key Facts ---- */\n  .kf-box { background: var(--card); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-top: 4px solid var(--accent);\n    border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px 4px; margin: 0 0 24px; font-size: 0.86rem; line-height: 1.42; }\n  .kf-box summary { list-style: none; cursor: pointer; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; }\n  .kf-box summary::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }\n  .kf-box summary::after { content: \"Show\"; font-size: 0.8rem; color: var(--accent); font-weight: 600; }\n  .kf-box[open] summary::after { content: \"Hide\"; }\n  .kf-box h2 { border: 0; margin: 0; padding: 0; font-size: 1rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .04em; color: var(--muted); }\n  .kf-box ol { margin: 8px 0 6px; padding-left: 1.25em; }\n  .kf-box li { margin: 0 0 6px; }\n  .kf-box .kf-foot { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.78rem; margin: 2px 0 8px; }\n  .kf-fade { display: none; }\n  /* ---- Official field reference box ---- */\n  .fieldref { background: var(--quote); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-left: 4px solid var(--accent); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px 6px; margin: 0 0 22px; }\n  .fieldref .status { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.85rem; }\n  /* ---- Raw code blocks (TAF, METAR, etc.) ---- */\n  .raw-wrap { position: relative; margin: 0 0 8px; }\n  pre.taf-raw { background: var(--card); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px; overflow-x: auto; margin: 0;\n    font: 0.95rem/1.9 ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, \"Liberation Mono\", monospace;\n    scrollbar-width: thin; scrollbar-color: var(--muted) var(--line); }\n  pre.taf-raw::-webkit-scrollbar { height: 8px; }\n  pre.taf-raw::-webkit-scrollbar-track { background: var(--line); border-radius: 0 0 8px 8px; }\n  pre.taf-raw::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb { background: var(--muted); border-radius: 4px; }\n  pre.taf-raw:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--accent); outline-offset: 2px; }\n  pre.taf-raw .tk { color: inherit; }\n  .scroll-note { display: none; }\n  .decode-cap { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.88rem; margin: 0 0 18px; }\n  .table-intro { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.92rem; margin: 0 0 8px; text-align: left; }\n  /* ---- Tables ---- */\n  table.decode td:first-child { font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, \"Liberation Mono\", monospace; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }\n  table.decode tr:target td { background: var(--quote); }\n  table.times td, table.times th { white-space: nowrap; }\n  table.times th { white-space: normal; }\n  .table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; margin: 0 0 20px; }\n  .table-wrap table { margin: 0; }\n  code { font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, \"Liberation Mono\", monospace; font-size: 0.92em; }\n  /* ---- On-page glossary (no-JS fallback and tooltip anchor targets) ---- */\n  .glossary dl { margin: 0 0 16px; }\n  .glossary dt { font-size: 1rem; font-weight: 600; margin-top: 14px; }\n  .glossary dfn { font-style: normal; }\n  .glossary dt:target, .glossary dt:target + dd { background: var(--quote); }\n  .glossary dd { margin: 2px 0 0; font-size: 0.95rem; }\n  .glossary .src { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.85rem; }\n  /* ---- Below 600 px: stacked-card tables, scroll hint and fade on raw blocks ---- */\n  @media (max-width: 599.98px) {\n    table.stack, table.stack tbody, table.stack tr, table.stack td, table.stack th[scope=\"row\"] { display: block; width: 100%; }\n    table.stack thead { position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; overflow: hidden; clip: rect(0 0 0 0); clip-path: inset(50%); white-space: nowrap; }\n    table.stack tr { border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 8px; margin: 0 0 12px; overflow: hidden; }\n    table.stack td, table.stack th[scope=\"row\"] { border: 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); padding: 8px 12px; }\n    table.stack tr > :first-child { border-top: 0; }\n    table.stack th[scope=\"row\"], table.decode td:first-child { background: var(--card); font-weight: 600; }\n    table.stack td[data-label]::before { content: attr(data-label); display: block; font: 600 0.74rem/1.4 -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, \"Segoe UI\", Roboto, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;\n      text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .04em; color: var(--muted); margin-bottom: 2px; }\n    table.decode td:first-child::before { display: none; }\n    .table-wrap:has(table.stack) { overflow: visible; }\n    .scroll-note { display: block; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.82rem; margin: 0 0 6px; }\n    .raw-wrap::after { content: \"\"; position: absolute; top: 1px; right: 1px; bottom: 9px; width: 40px; pointer-events: none;\n      border-radius: 0 8px 0 0; background: linear-gradient(to right, rgba(0,0,0,0), var(--card)); transition: opacity .15s; }\n    .raw-wrap.at-end::after { opacity: 0; }\n    table.times { font-size: 0.88rem; }\n    table.times th, table.times td { padding: 8px 7px; }\n  }\n  /* ---- Desktop: two columns, sticky Key Facts (kept last so it overrides the mobile toggle styles) ---- */\n  @media (min-width: 980px) {\n    .hub { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 760px) 320px; justify-content: center; }\n    .hub-head { grid-column: 1; grid-row: 1; }\n    .hub-body { grid-column: 1; grid-row: 2; }\n    .keyfacts { grid-column: 2; grid-row: 1 / span 2; }\n    /* Capped only when taller than the viewport; a bottom fade shows while more facts are below. */\n    .keyfacts .kf-box { position: sticky; top: 16px; max-height: calc(100vh - 32px); overflow-y: auto; }\n    .keyfacts summary { pointer-events: none; }\n    .keyfacts .kf-box summary::after, .keyfacts .kf-box[open] summary::after { content: none; }\n    .kf-box.is-capped .kf-fade { display: block; position: sticky; bottom: -4px; height: 36px; margin: -36px -16px 0; pointer-events: none;\n      background: linear-gradient(to bottom, rgba(0,0,0,0), var(--card)); }\n    .kf-box.is-capped.at-end .kf-fade { opacity: 0; }\n  }\n  /* ---- Glossary tooltip (hover, keyboard focus, tap). No framework. ---- */\n  a.gl { color: inherit; text-decoration: underline dotted; text-decoration-thickness: 1px; text-underline-offset: 3px; cursor: help; }\n  a.gl:hover, a.gl[aria-expanded=\"true\"] { color: var(--accent); text-decoration-style: solid; }\n  a.gl:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--accent); outline-offset: 2px; border-radius: 2px; }\n  .gl-tip { position: fixed; z-index: 1000; max-width: min(340px, calc(100vw - 24px)); background: var(--bg); color: var(--fg);\n    border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 6px 24px rgba(0,0,0,.18); padding: 10px 12px;\n    font: 0.88rem/1.5 -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, \"Segoe UI\", Roboto, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif; white-space: normal; text-align: left; }\n  .gl-tip[hidden] { display: none; }\n  .gl-tip .gl-t { display: block; font-weight: 700; }\n  .gl-tip .gl-x { display: block; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.82rem; margin-bottom: 4px; }\n  .gl-tip .gl-d { display: block; margin-bottom: 6px; }\n  .gl-tip .gl-s { display: block; font-size: 0.8rem; }\n  .gl-tip .gl-s a { color: var(--accent); }\n  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) { .gl-tip { transition: opacity .12s; } }\n"
